{"externally_confirmed_facts":["`javax.crypto.Cipher` is the JCA/JCE class for encryption and decryption and also supports key wrapping and unwrapping. A caller obtains it through `Cipher.getInstance`, whose transformation argument identifies the requested algorithm and, where applicable, mode and padding.","The reported value `ChaCha20-Poly1305` refers to the transformation string passed to `Cipher.getInstance`, not to a generic operation named "ChaCha20-Poly1305." Java's Standard Algorithm Names specification identifies it as ChaCha20 in AEAD mode with Poly1305, and the Java SE 25 `Cipher` API lists it as a transformation that every Java platform implementation must support.","The official `Cipher` documentation recommends fully specifying algorithm, mode, and padding when those components apply, rather than depending on provider defaults, because defaults may vary by provider. A `Cipher` must be initialized for the intended operation before `update` or `doFinal`.","For encryption where initialization generates needed parameters, `Cipher` documents retrieving them with `getParameters()` or `getIV()`. For decryption or unwrapping, if required parameters are unavailable, initialization fails; the API does not specify silently generating substitute parameters.","For AEAD transformations, AAD must be supplied before ciphertext is processed. Encryption appends an authentication tag at finalization, and decryption verifies that tag at finalization.","For ChaCha20 and ChaCha20-Poly1305, the `Cipher` Javadoc requires a unique nonce for each operation with a given key and directs callers to re-initialize after each encryption or decryption operation with parameters that specify a different nonce. The general statement that `doFinal` resets the cipher does not remove this ChaCha20/ChaCha20-Poly1305 re-initialization requirement.","The official `Cipher` and Standard Algorithm Names sources do not prescribe replacing `ChaCha20-Poly1305` with AES or another transformation because an external rule rejects it. Their documented guidance is to use a supported, explicit transformation and initialize it with the necessary key, parameters, and, when appropriate, randomness; for ChaCha20-Poly1305, that includes nonce uniqueness and re-initialization for every operation.","`Cipher` specifies no universal persisted-ciphertext or wire-record format, no encoding of cipher parameters, and no generic migration path between transformations. It documents obtaining encryption parameters through `getParameters()` or `getIV()`, and AEAD tag behavior, but does not prescribe how an application serializes, versions, transmits, or negotiates transformation names, parameters, tags, or AAD.","The official sources provide no mechanism by which ciphertext produced under one transformation becomes decryptable under a different transformation. Compatibility of an existing persisted or wire format is therefore not determined by the `Cipher` API alone.","`ChaCha20-Poly1305` was listed in the Java SE 11 Standard Algorithm Names specification and is listed by Java SE 25 as a required `Cipher` transformation. Specific runtime/provider compatibility still depends on the runtime version and installed provider(s); implementations may support additional algorithms and document that support separately.","The primary CrySL source declares its governed API type exactly as `SPEC javax.crypto.Cipher`. Its factory-event matching clause is quoted verbatim as:
```
g1: getInstance(transformation);
g2: getInstance(transformation, _);
Get := g1 | g2;
```
Thus, `Get` models the one-argument `getInstance(transformation)` form and a two-argument form whose second argument is represented by `_`.","The same CrySL source models the method names `getInstance`, `init`, `updateAAD`, `update`, `doFinal`, and `wrap`; it does not define an `unwrap` event. Its modeled forms constrain parameter lists: `getInstance` has the two forms quoted above; `init`, `updateAAD`, `update`, and `doFinal` are limited to listed forms; and wrapping is modeled as `wrappedKeyBytes = wrap(wrappedKey)`.","Under the source's declared scope, the rule governs `javax.crypto.Cipher`; the source does not declare an alternative receiver type. Therefore, as stated by that source, a method is not within this rule merely because it shares a modeled name such as `getInstance`, `init`, `update`, or `doFinal`; the declaration scopes the specification to `javax.crypto.Cipher`.","After capturing `transformation`, the CrySL rule evaluates conditions involving `alg(transformation)`, `mode(transformation)`, and `pad(transformation)`, as well as initialization mode, input-range constraints, and named predicates. Its reported transformation policy admits listed AES, RSA, and PBE-with-AES cases under stated restrictions, but does not list `ChaCha20-Poly1305`. This establishes that the transformation is outside that CrySL policy; it does not establish that it is invalid under the official `Cipher` API.","The detector-message utility name `CipherTransformationUtil` is not established by the cited CrySL rule source. The rule source alone does not document that utility, the analyzer's type-resolution behavior, the mapping of rule conditions to the reported diagnostic, or the compiled rule artifact used by the detector."],"sources":[{"relevance":"Official API semantics for `Cipher`: transformations, initialization, generated parameters and IVs, AEAD ordering/tag behavior, ChaCha20/ChaCha20-Poly1305 nonce and re-initialization requirements, and Java SE required transformations.","limitations":"This is Java SE 25 documentation. It does not identify the runtime version or provider configuration of any particular application, and it does not define an application persistence or wire format.","claim":"Documents correct `javax.crypto.Cipher` use and lists `ChaCha20-Poly1305` among transformations every Java platform implementation must support.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/api/java.base/javax/crypto/Cipher.html"},{"relevance":"Official standard naming and transformation-support information, including provider extensibility.","limitations":"Standard names and required-platform support do not establish which provider is installed or selected in a particular runtime.","claim":"Identifies standard cryptographic names and states that implementations may support additional algorithms.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/25/docs/specs/security/standard-names.html"},{"relevance":"Historical Java SE version-support evidence for `ChaCha20-Poly1305`.","limitations":"This establishes Java SE 11 documentation support, not an application's actual minimum Java version or installed provider support.","claim":"Lists `ChaCha20-Poly1305` in the Java SE 11 Standard Algorithm Names specification.","authority":"official","url":"https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/11/docs/specs/security/standard-names.html"},{"relevance":"Primary source for the exact CrySL specification declaration, event matching forms, and rule conditions discussed in the finding.","limitations":"The URL is a mutable `refs/heads/master` reference. The supplied evidence does not independently verify it byte-for-byte against the stated SHA-256 or against the detector's compiled rule artifact. The rule does not document `CipherTransformationUtil` or analyzer implementation behavior.","claim":"Declares `SPEC javax.crypto.Cipher`; defines modeled `getInstance`, `init`, `updateAAD`, `update`, `doFinal`, and `wrap` events; and applies its transformation and predicate restrictions.","authority":"primary","url":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/CROSSINGTUD/Crypto-API-Rules/refs/heads/master/JavaCryptographicArchitecture/src/Cipher.crysl"}],"queries_performed":["Reviewed the supplied research evidence for the official Java SE `javax.crypto.Cipher` API documentation, Java SE Standard Algorithm Names specifications, and the primary `Cipher.crysl` rule source.","Compared the documented `Cipher.getInstance` transformation semantics and Java SE transformation support with the CrySL rule's declared `javax.crypto.Cipher` scope and reported transformation restrictions."],"externally_unresolved_questions":["The target application's Java runtime baseline, selected/installed JCA providers, and whether the relevant runtime supports the intended transformation cannot be determined from these sources.","The persisted-data or wire-format specification, including how transformation identifiers, parameters/nonces, AEAD tags, and AAD are represented or versioned, was not supplied. The official `Cipher` API does not define those formats or a transformation-migration procedure.","The fetched mutable CrySL source was not independently verified against rule SHA-256 `da73b53a10fc760da823ae81250e204a4d20a0566a38603e1938db9489d6ad92` or the detector's compiled rules artifact.","The CrySL source does not establish the implementation or semantics of `CipherTransformationUtil`, the detector's resolution behavior, or why the detector reports this particular wording."]}
